private fun runMember(
    name: String, config: Map<String, Any>, topic: String, handover: Boolean,
    owned: MutableMap<String, Set<Int>>, revoked: MutableMap<String, Int>, processed: AtomicLong, stop: AtomicBoolean,
) {
    try {
        memberLoop(name, config, topic, handover, owned, revoked, processed, stop)
    } catch (e: KafkaException) {
        if (!stop.get()) throw e // closing while the group still moves partitions is harmless at the end of a demo
    }
}

private fun memberLoop(
    name: String, config: Map<String, Any>, topic: String, handover: Boolean,
    owned: MutableMap<String, Set<Int>>, revoked: MutableMap<String, Int>, processed: AtomicLong, stop: AtomicBoolean,
) {
    KafkaConsumer<String, String>(config).use { c ->
        c.subscribe(listOf(topic), object : ConsumerRebalanceListener {
            override fun onPartitionsRevoked(partitions: Collection<TopicPartition>) {
                if (partitions.isEmpty()) return
                // the loop below processes every record it polls, so the position is how far this member got
                if (handover) c.commitSync(partitions.associateWith { OffsetAndMetadata(c.position(it)) })
                if (!stop.get()) {                       // closing at the end: commit, but stay quiet
                    revoked.merge(name, partitions.size, Int::plus)
                    log("$name revoked  ${partitions.map { it.partition() }.sorted()}" +
                        if (handover) ", committed ${partitions.sortedBy { it.partition() }.map { c.position(it) }}" else ", committed nothing")
                }
                owned[name] = owned[name].orEmpty() - partitions.map { it.partition() }.toSet()
            }

            override fun onPartitionsAssigned(partitions: Collection<TopicPartition>) {
                val now = c.assignment().map { it.partition() }.toSet()
                if (partitions.isNotEmpty()) {
                    val from = partitions.sortedBy { it.partition() }.map { c.position(it) } // the committed offsets
                    log("$name assigned ${partitions.map { it.partition() }.sorted()}, starts at $from, owns ${now.sorted()}")
                }
                owned[name] = now
            }
        })
        while (!stop.get()) processed.addAndGet(c.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100)).count().toLong()) // "processing" is free here
    }
}

sections.body:
Whenever a member joins or leaves, partitions change owner. That is a **rebalance**. The partition moves, but what about its bookmark?

Take partition 3. c1 owns it and has processed up to offset 341. It commits after every batch of 100, so the group's committed offset is 300. Now the group gives partition 3 to c2. c2 starts where the group's bookmark says, at 300, and processes 300 to 340 a second time. With the default `max.poll.records` of 500, one rebalance can replay up to a whole batch. And if c1 is still working through its batch when c2 starts, both apply the same payments at the same moment.

The fix is to tell c1 before the partition leaves. You register a `ConsumerRebalanceListener` with `subscribe(topics, listener)`, and the consumer calls it from inside `poll`, on your polling thread:

- `onPartitionsRevoked(partitions)`: these partitions are leaving, and they are still yours. Finish or abandon the current work on them and commit how far you got. This is the handover.
- `onPartitionsAssigned(partitions)`: these are new. The consumer will start at their committed offsets. If you keep offsets in your own database, `seek` here instead.
- `onPartitionsLost(partitions)`: the group already gave these to someone else, usually because this member was thrown out (section 4). Don't commit: the new owner's bookmark is no longer yours to move.

```java
consumer.subscribe(List.of("payments"), new ConsumerRebalanceListener() {
    public void onPartitionsRevoked(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        consumer.commitSync(processedOffsets(partitions));   // the next owner starts exactly here
    }
    public void onPartitionsAssigned(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) { }
    public void onPartitionsLost(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        forget(partitions);                                  // not ours any more: no commit
    }
});
```

```kotlin
consumer.subscribe(listOf("payments"), object : ConsumerRebalanceListener {
    override fun onPartitionsRevoked(partitions: Collection<TopicPartition>) {
        consumer.commitSync(processedOffsets(partitions))    // the next owner starts exactly here
    }
    override fun onPartitionsAssigned(partitions: Collection<TopicPartition>) {}
    override fun onPartitionsLost(partitions: Collection<TopicPartition>) {
        forget(partitions)                                   // not ours any more: no commit
    }
})
```

`processedOffsets` is yours to write: the next offset to read of each partition, counting only records that are fully processed. That is lesson 6's rule, last processed plus one. If the loop processes everything it polls before it polls again, `consumer.position(tp)` is that number.

The handover is safe because of an ordering rule. The group never gives a partition to its new owner until the old owner has revoked it. So c1's commit in `onPartitionsRevoked` always lands before c2 reads the committed offset. A rebalance can still repeat records, if a member dies without revoking, which is why the ledger from lesson 6 stays idempotent. But a planned rebalance repeats nothing.

